FINAL IVX REELS + MODULE DATA RENDERING STABILITY
DEEP QA, ROOT-CAUSE FIX, INSTAGRAM-STYLE EXPERIENCE, DEPLOY WITH PROOF

EXECUTE NOW — DO NOT RETURN ONLY A REPORT
DO NOT ADD NEW FEATURES
DO NOT REDESIGN THE ENTIRE APP
FIX THE EXISTING REELS CRASH AND STANDARDIZE HOW EVERY MODULE LOADS, CACHES, RENDERS, REFRESHES, AND RECOVERS

==================================================
PRIMARY OBJECTIVES
==================================================

1. Reproduce and permanently fix the Reels crash that occurs after several seconds or several reel transitions.

2. Remove blank screens, full-screen loading states, visual jumping, repeated spinners, and delayed data replacement across every IVX module.

3. Implement an Instagram-style user experience pattern:

- cached content appears immediately;
- fresh data loads in the background;
- skeletons are used only where no cached content exists;
- lists render incrementally;
- media is prefetched intelligently;
- one failing item does not crash the complete screen;
- scroll position is preserved;
- refresh never clears valid visible content;
- network recovery happens automatically;
- memory is released when media leaves the viewport.

4. Apply one reusable data-display architecture across all modules.

==================================================
CRITICAL RULES
==================================================

Do not claim the crash is fixed from source inspection only.

Do not use one successful launch as proof.

Do not hide errors behind a generic Retry screen.

Do not solve the crash by disabling Reels, lowering quality permanently, or removing core behavior.

Do not show fake data while real data loads.

Do not clear the existing list before refresh completes.

Do not mount every video simultaneously.

Do not allow one malformed reel, card, image, transaction, investor, or API row to crash the entire route.

Every failure must include:

- exact route;
- exact component;
- exact file;
- exact function;
- trace ID;
- error message;
- device state;
- memory state;
- network state;
- active reel/module ID;
- corrective action;
- regression test.

==================================================
PHASE 1 — REPRODUCE THE REELS CRASH
==================================================

Reproduce on a physical Android device using the current production APK.

Run these scenarios separately:

1. Open Reels and remain idle for 10 minutes.
2. Swipe through 25 reels.
3. Swipe through 100 reels.
4. Swipe quickly through 50 reels.
5. Scroll forward and backward repeatedly.
6. Background the app during playback.
7. Return after 5 seconds.
8. Return after 1 minute.
9. Turn the screen off and back on.
10. Switch Wi-Fi to cellular.
11. Disable network during playback.
12. Restore network.
13. Open comments and return.
14. Open a property from a reel and return.
15. Rotate the device if rotation is supported.
16. Receive a notification while playing.
17. Open Reels after using Portfolio and Chat.
18. Open Reels with low available memory.
19. Test a reel with a missing thumbnail.
20. Test a reel with an invalid or expired video URL.
21. Test an unsupported media codec.
22. Test a deleted database row.
23. Test a partial API response.
24. Test an empty list.
25. Test pagination failure.

For every crash capture:

- timestamp;
- Android model;
- Android version;
- APK version;
- route;
- reel ID;
- media URL domain;
- player state;
- current index;
- previous index;
- next index;
- app lifecycle state;
- JS exception;
- native exception;
- memory before crash;
- memory after crash;
- player count;
- active network request count;
- trace ID.

Required proof:

CRASH REPRODUCED: YES / NO
REPRODUCTION STEPS:
ROOT CAUSE:
AFFECTED FILE:
AFFECTED FUNCTION:

==================================================
PHASE 2 — ADD REAL CRASH OBSERVABILITY
==================================================

Wire production-safe crash diagnostics for Reels and all high-risk modules.

Capture:

- unhandled JavaScript exceptions;
- React error-boundary events;
- native video-player errors;
- media decode failures;
- out-of-memory warnings;
- stalled playback;
- API timeouts;
- malformed records;
- render-loop detection;
- navigation failures;
- app background/foreground transitions.

Every event must include:

- traceId;
- user role, redacted;
- route;
- module;
- entity ID;
- app version;
- build number;
- source commit;
- device model;
- operating system;
- network type;
- memory usage;
- event timestamp.

Do not log:

- passwords;
- access tokens;
- refresh tokens;
- service-role keys;
- private investor documents;
- full tax or identity information.

==================================================
PHASE 3 — REELS PLAYER ARCHITECTURE
==================================================

Refactor Reels so only a very small media window remains active.

Required behavior:

CURRENT ITEM:
- mounted;
- playing;
- audio active;
- progress active.

PREVIOUS ITEM:
- optionally mounted;
- paused;
- audio released.

NEXT ITEM:
- optionally prefetched;
- paused;
- no unnecessary playback instance.

ALL OTHER ITEMS:
- video player released;
- listeners removed;
- timers removed;
- network requests cancelled when safe;
- thumbnails may remain cached.

Target active player count:

Maximum 2 or 3.

Never create one player for every reel in the list.

On index change:

1. Pause previous player.
2. Remove progress listeners.
3. Release obsolete player instance.
4. Activate current player.
5. Prefetch next media.
6. Cancel stale requests.
7. Persist current index.
8. Record playback error without crashing the screen.

==================================================
PHASE 4 — VIDEO LIFECYCLE SAFETY
==================================================

Audit and fix:

- player creation;
- player disposal;
- event listeners;
- time-update listeners;
- autoplay;
- mute state;
- app-state listeners;
- focus listeners;
- navigation listeners;
- retry timers;
- pagination timers;
- prefetch operations;
- thumbnail loading;
- comments subscriptions.

All listeners and timers must have cleanup logic.

Use route focus state:

- play only when route is focused;
- pause when another screen opens;
- release expensive resources when route remains unfocused;
- restore safely when the user returns.

Use app lifecycle:

ACTIVE:
- resume only the currently visible reel.

BACKGROUND:
- pause playback;
- suspend progress timers;
- avoid duplicate player recreation.

RETURN TO ACTIVE:
- validate the active URL;
- resume from a safe state;
- do not instantiate duplicate players.

==================================================
PHASE 5 — MEDIA URL RELIABILITY
==================================================

Before playback, verify media records contain:

- reel ID;
- valid URL;
- MIME type;
- media status;
- width;
- height;
- duration when known;
- thumbnail;
- publication status.

For private or expiring URLs:

- refresh signed URLs before expiry;
- do not cache expired signatures indefinitely;
- retry URL refresh once;
- show a per-item error card if refresh fails.

A failed reel must show:

“Video temporarily unavailable”

with:

- Retry;
- Continue to next reel;
- Report issue.

Do not crash the complete Reels route.

==================================================
PHASE 6 — REELS MEMORY CONTROL
==================================================

Measure memory during:

- initial open;
- 10 reels;
- 25 reels;
- 50 reels;
- 100 reels;
- backward scrolling;
- background and return.

Audit:

- video player count;
- decoded frame buffers;
- image cache;
- thumbnail cache;
- array growth;
- retained list items;
- event listeners;
- closures retaining media data;
- comments and like subscriptions;
- analytics queues.

Acceptance:

- memory reaches a stable range instead of growing indefinitely;
- released players are garbage-collectable;
- no increasing listener count;
- no increasing timer count;
- no duplicate subscription count;
- no crash after a 60-minute soak test.

==================================================
PHASE 7 — REELS LIST CONFIGURATION
==================================================

Use a virtualized list correctly.

Verify:

- stable keyExtractor using reel ID;
- no array index as primary key;
- fixed or estimated item dimensions;
- one full-screen reel per page;
- paging enabled;
- removeClippedSubviews evaluated on actual devices;
- initial render count kept small;
- render batch size bounded;
- window size bounded;
- memoized reel item;
- stable callbacks;
- no new object/function creation causing every card to rerender;
- viewability config is stable;
- pagination is cursor-based;
- duplicate pages are rejected.

Do not rerender every reel because one like count, mute state, or progress value changes.

Keep high-frequency playback progress local to the active reel component.

==================================================
PHASE 8 — INSTAGRAM-STYLE DATA EXPERIENCE
==================================================

Apply the following pattern across all modules:

CACHE FIRST
→ DISPLAY IMMEDIATELY
→ BACKGROUND REFRESH
→ MERGE NEW DATA
→ PRESERVE SCROLL
→ SHOW SMALL REFRESH INDICATOR
→ KEEP OLD CONTENT IF REFRESH FAILS

The app must not display a full-screen spinner every time a user opens a module.

Required state model:

- idle;
- loadingInitial;
- showingCached;
- refreshing;
- loadingMore;
- success;
- empty;
- partialError;
- fatalError.

Rules:

INITIAL LOAD WITH NO CACHE:
- show skeleton layout matching the final screen.

INITIAL LOAD WITH CACHE:
- show cache immediately;
- refresh silently.

PULL TO REFRESH:
- retain existing content;
- show refresh control;
- replace/merge only after success.

PAGINATION:
- keep existing rows;
- show small footer loader;
- do not block the screen.

PARTIAL API FAILURE:
- keep valid sections visible;
- show inline retry for failed section.

FATAL AUTH OR SCHEMA FAILURE:
- show controlled error state with trace ID.

==================================================
PHASE 9 — CENTRAL QUERY AND CACHE LAYER
==================================================

Create one reusable module-data layer for:

- query keys;
- cache;
- stale times;
- retries;
- pagination;
- deduplication;
- optimistic updates;
- background refetch;
- offline persistence where appropriate;
- cancellation;
- error normalization;
- telemetry.

Each request must have a stable query key including:

- module;
- user/role scope;
- filters;
- sort;
- cursor;
- entity ID where applicable.

Prevent:

- duplicate requests from multiple mounted components;
- fetch-on-every-render;
- fetch loops;
- race conditions;
- stale response overwriting newer data;
- old screen request updating a new route;
- loading-state flicker.

Use request cancellation or stale-response guards when routes change.

==================================================
PHASE 10 — NORMALIZE EVERY API RESPONSE
==================================================

Every module must use a defined runtime schema.

Validate API data before rendering.

Never directly assume:

data.totalBalance
data.totalDeposited
data.items
data.user.name
data.property.images

exists.

Normalize missing values safely:

- arrays → [];
- optional objects → null;
- optional numbers → null or documented zero only when zero is semantically correct;
- optional strings → null or empty display text;
- invalid records → excluded and logged;
- missing critical identifiers → inline invalid-record state.

Important:

Do not convert “unknown” financial values into `$0` unless the database confirms zero.

Use:

Unknown
Not available
Not calculated

when the value is absent.

==================================================
PHASE 11 — MODULE-BY-MODULE RENDERING AUDIT
==================================================

Audit these modules individually:

1. Home
2. Invest
3. Market
4. Portfolio
5. Wallet
6. Transactions
7. IVX IA Chat
8. Messages
9. Reels
10. Members
11. Investors
12. Buyers
13. CRM
14. Properties
15. Deals
16. Documents
17. Media
18. Notifications
19. Owner Dashboard
20. Admin Hub
21. Executive Layer
22. Autonomous Dashboard
23. Reports
24. Settings
25. Variables
26. Landing management

For every module document:

- route;
- data source;
- API endpoint;
- database tables;
- query key;
- cache policy;
- initial state;
- loading state;
- empty state;
- partial-error state;
- fatal-error state;
- refresh behavior;
- pagination behavior;
- offline behavior;
- retry behavior;
- scroll restoration;
- memory risk;
- crash boundary;
- tests.

==================================================
PHASE 12 — HOME / DASHBOARD MODULES
==================================================

Do not wait for every dashboard panel before displaying the screen.

Load panels independently:

- headline metrics;
- deals;
- investors;
- activities;
- notifications;
- health;
- autonomous activity.

If one panel fails:

- render the other panels;
- show inline retry for the failed panel;
- do not show a full-screen failure.

Skeletons must match card dimensions to avoid layout jumping.

==================================================
PHASE 13 — PORTFOLIO / WALLET / FINANCIAL DATA
==================================================

Fix the previous undefined crashes permanently.

Audit all values:

- totalPortfolioValue;
- availableBalance;
- totalBalance;
- totalDeposited;
- totalWithdrawn;
- earnings;
- holdings;
- transactions;
- pendingFunds;
- distributions.

Create one normalized financial view model.

Required behavior:

- missing provider → controlled unavailable state;
- missing record → empty state;
- unknown value → “Unknown,” not fabricated zero;
- zero confirmed by ledger → `$0.00`;
- stale data → display last updated time;
- refresh → retain visible values until replacement arrives;
- one bad transaction → skip/log that row, not crash entire screen.

==================================================
PHASE 14 — CHAT MODULE
==================================================

Apply cache-first rendering:

- show latest locally cached messages immediately;
- load newest server messages in background;
- merge by stable message ID;
- deduplicate;
- preserve chronological order;
- preserve scroll position;
- open at the latest message;
- paginate older messages upward;
- do not clear the conversation on refresh;
- do not show a full-screen spinner after initial cache exists.

For a running autonomous task:

- restore task card from persistent local state;
- reconcile with server status;
- update one card;
- never create duplicate cards.

==================================================
PHASE 15 — MEMBERS / INVESTORS / BUYERS / CRM
==================================================

Use:

- paginated lists;
- server-side filtering;
- server-side sorting;
- stable entity IDs;
- cached first page;
- background refresh;
- optimistic local edit with rollback;
- inline row errors;
- preserved search text;
- preserved scroll position;
- accurate totals from the server.

Do not load thousands of records into one unbounded array.

Do not replace the full list after editing one record.

==================================================
PHASE 16 — PROPERTIES / DEALS
==================================================

For cards:

- thumbnail first;
- full image after;
- fixed image aspect ratio;
- no layout shift;
- cached card metadata;
- image fallback;
- last-updated timestamp;
- stale-data indicator if needed.

Property gallery:

- render only nearby images;
- prefetch the next 1–2;
- release distant full-resolution images;
- preserve selected image index;
- failed image does not crash card.

==================================================
PHASE 17 — DOCUMENTS / MEDIA / UPLOADS
==================================================

Upload flow must show:

- validating;
- uploading;
- processing;
- completed;
- failed;
- retrying.

Use resumable or safe retry where supported.

Do not duplicate uploads after retry.

Validate:

- file type;
- MIME type;
- size;
- extension;
- ownership;
- storage path;
- virus/security policy where available.

Use thumbnail/preview instead of loading full files into memory.

==================================================
PHASE 18 — NOTIFICATIONS
==================================================

Load cached notifications immediately.

Background refresh unread count.

Mark-read operations:

- optimistic update;
- rollback on failure;
- no full-screen loading;
- no duplicated notification;
- stable notification ID.

==================================================
PHASE 19 — ERROR BOUNDARIES
==================================================

Implement boundaries at three levels:

APP BOUNDARY:
- only for unrecoverable global failure.

ROUTE BOUNDARY:
- isolates one module.

CARD/ITEM BOUNDARY:
- isolates one malformed reel, chart, transaction, or card.

A single failed reel must not crash Reels.

A single bad metric must not crash Executive Layer.

A single bad transaction must not crash Portfolio.

Each boundary must record:

- route;
- component;
- entity ID;
- trace ID;
- error;
- source commit.

==================================================
PHASE 20 — SKELETON AND EMPTY-STATE SYSTEM
==================================================

Create shared components:

- ListSkeleton;
- CardSkeleton;
- MetricSkeleton;
- ReelSkeleton;
- ChatSkeleton;
- ProfileSkeleton;
- TableSkeleton;
- InlineRetry;
- EmptyState;
- StaleDataBanner;
- OfflineBanner.

Do not use a generic spinner as the primary module-loading UI.

Skeleton dimensions must match real content to prevent jumping.

==================================================
PHASE 21 — PREFETCH STRATEGY
==================================================

Prefetch only high-probability next actions.

Examples:

- next reel;
- next property image;
- first page of a tab the user is likely to open;
- selected deal detail;
- latest chat messages.

Do not prefetch:

- every video;
- every property gallery;
- every admin module;
- thousands of member rows.

Use network awareness:

- Wi-Fi: normal prefetch;
- cellular: reduced prefetch;
- data saver: thumbnails only;
- offline: cache only.

==================================================
PHASE 22 — NETWORK RESILIENCE
==================================================

Implement:

- bounded retries;
- exponential backoff;
- request timeout by endpoint type;
- offline detection;
- online recovery;
- request cancellation;
- idempotency for writes;
- stale-response rejection;
- retry only safe requests automatically.

Do not retry:

- invalid credentials;
- authorization failures;
- validation failures;
- destructive writes without idempotency.

==================================================
PHASE 23 — PERFORMANCE INSTRUMENTATION
==================================================

Measure per module:

- time to first visible content;
- time to fresh content;
- API p50/p95/p99;
- render duration;
- rerender count;
- list dropped frames;
- memory;
- CPU;
- network bytes;
- cache hit rate;
- crash-free sessions.

Targets for production testing:

- cached content visible: under 300 ms where cache exists;
- skeleton visible: under 300 ms where cache is empty;
- no long blank screen;
- no repeated full-screen loaders;
- list interactions remain responsive;
- media transitions do not freeze UI;
- crash-free Reels soak test: 60 minutes.

These are internal targets, not guarantees. Report actual measurements.

==================================================
PHASE 24 — AUTOMATED TESTS
==================================================

Add tests for:

REELS:
- only current/adjacent players mounted;
- player released after item leaves window;
- failed media does not crash route;
- expired URL refresh;
- empty reels list;
- duplicate pagination page;
- rapid swipe;
- background/foreground;
- network loss/recovery;
- malformed reel;
- memory/listener cleanup.

DATA LAYER:
- cache-first rendering;
- background refresh;
- stale response ignored;
- duplicate request deduped;
- pagination merge;
- refresh preserves data;
- partial API failure;
- schema normalization;
- request cancellation.

MODULES:
- empty state;
- loading state;
- cached state;
- refresh state;
- partial-error state;
- fatal-error state;
- malformed row;
- offline state;
- retry state.

Run:

- targeted unit tests;
- integration tests;
- React rendering tests;
- API contract tests;
- TypeScript;
- lint;
- Android smoke tests.

==================================================
PHASE 25 — PHYSICAL DEVICE QA
==================================================

Android minimum:

1. Install clean APK.
2. Open each module.
3. Confirm immediate skeleton or cached content.
4. Confirm no blank screen.
5. Confirm no layout jump.
6. Refresh every module.
7. Test offline.
8. Restore network.
9. Background and return.
10. Restart app.
11. Verify cached data returns.
12. Verify fresh data merges.
13. Run Reels for 60 minutes.
14. Swipe through at least 200 transitions.
15. Open/close comments 25 times.
16. Open property from reel and return 25 times.
17. Confirm zero crash.
18. Record memory trend.
19. Record dropped frames.
20. Capture screenshots and logs.

Repeat equivalent tests on iOS when available.

==================================================
PHASE 26 — DEPLOYMENT
==================================================

After all critical fixes pass:

1. Commit only related files.
2. Run targeted tests.
3. Run full regression.
4. Run typecheck.
5. Run lint.
6. Build Android APK.
7. Deploy backend if required.
8. Verify GitHub/Render/runtime parity.
9. Install APK on physical Android.
10. Repeat Reels soak test.
11. Repeat module-loading QA.
12. Roll back automatically if crash rate increases.

Owner approval required for:

CONFIRM_IVX_GITHUB_WRITE
CONFIRM_IVX_RENDER_DEPLOY
CONFIRM_IVX_APK_UPLOAD

==================================================
FINAL ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
==================================================

Do not mark complete until:

[ ] Reels crash is reproduced before the fix.
[ ] Exact root cause is documented.
[ ] Reels does not crash during a 60-minute device soak test.
[ ] At least 200 reel transitions complete.
[ ] Player count remains bounded.
[ ] Memory does not grow indefinitely.
[ ] Listeners and timers are cleaned up.
[ ] One malformed reel cannot crash the route.
[ ] Cached module data displays immediately.
[ ] Background refresh does not clear visible content.
[ ] Full-screen spinners are removed from normal cached flows.
[ ] Skeletons match final layouts.
[ ] Scroll positions are preserved.
[ ] Pagination is bounded and deduplicated.
[ ] Financial undefined-value crashes are eliminated.
[ ] Every module has loading, empty, partial-error, and fatal-error states.
[ ] Android physical-device QA passes.
[ ] Full regression passes.
[ ] APK is built and installed.
[ ] Production commit parity is true.
[ ] Live crash telemetry shows no new Reels crash after release.

